  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-armatus",
      "html": "<p>The tyre-track eel, Mastacembelus armatus, is a spiny eel from South Asia and Myanmar, also known as the white-spotted spiny eel. Growing to around 91 cm, it hides during the day and often buries itself, so it needs sand, plenty of hiding places and a close-fitting hood. It eats live fish in the wild, and tankmates should be too large to be considered prey.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-armatus",
      "html": "<p>Asia: Pakistan, India, Nepal, Sri Lanka, Bangladesh and Myanmar.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-armatus",
      "html": "<p>Its habitat ranges from highland streams to lowland wetlands, and it is usually found in streams and rivers over sand, pebbles or boulders. It seldom leaves the bottom unless disturbed, and sometimes lies partly buried in fine sediment. It also lives in still water, including coastal marshes and the reservoirs of Sri Lanka's dry zone.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-armatus",
      "html": "<p>Standard length 910 mm (36 in). Aquarium base 120 × 60 cm (48 × 24 in). Volume about 432 litres (114 US gal), for a tank of 120 × 60 × 60 cm. It can be kept in a smaller tank when juvenile.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-armatus",
      "html": "<p>Tank should be well planted with floating plants also used. An abundance of hiding places should be provided as this species likes to hide away during the day. Bogwood, rock caves and PVC piping are all suitable for this purpose. Sand should be used as substrate as the spiny eels often like to bury themselves. Dimmer lighting will encourage the fish to venture from its hiding places more often. A close-fitting hood is required as the eel can find its way through the smallest of gaps. Water flow should be fairly gentle as the fish mainly inhabits areas of still water in the wild. This species does best when kept in mildly brackish water.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-armatus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 24–28°C (75–82°F)</li><li>pH: 6.0–7.0</li><li>Hardness: 5–12 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-armatus",
      "html": "<p>Prefers meaty foods such as prawn, krill, and lancefish. Live and frozen foods can also be fed, however these foods alone are unlikely to be enough to sustain larger fish. Some specimens will also accept vegetable matter, although this is fairly rare.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-armatus",
      "html": "<p>Generally peaceful community fish, although care should be taken to ensure tankmates are too large to be considered prey as this species does eat live fish in the wild. It is recommended to only keep one per tank as they may be aggressive to conspecifics. This can sometimes be avoided by keeping the fish in groups of five or more individuals.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-armatus",
      "html": "<p>Female is fuller bodied when in breeding condition.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-armatus",
      "html": "<p>No reports of breeding in the hobby.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mastacembelus-armatus",
      "html": "<p>The tyre track eel is also referred to as the white-spotted spiny eel. A member of the spiny eel family, which range across tropical Asia and Africa, Mastacembelus armatus is one of the more common species available to the hobby, along with the peacock eel (Macrognathus siamensis) and the fire eel (Mastacembelus erythrotaenia). These species are all from Asia but there are some members of the family from Africa that are occasionally seen for sale. The majority of these species are from the Afromastacembelus genus, with the most common representative being the Tanganyikan spiny eel (Afromastacembelus tanganicae). The family is named 'spiny eels' due to the presence of spines running along the back of the fish. These are harmless to humans.</p>"
    }
  ],
